[00:02:53.489] Browser LOG     Fetched data: [{"_embedded":{"author":[{"_links":"[Object]","avatar_urls":"[Object]","description":"","id":2,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/author/oyonews/","name":"oyo news","slug":"oyonews","url":"https://oyonews.com.ng"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"_links":"[Object]","alt_text":"","author":2,"caption":"[Object]","date":"2026-07-05T08:22:49","featured_media":0,"id":95,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/appclick-ict-academy-unveils-ibadan-headquarters-graduates-over-60-students/fb_img_1783239986189/#main","media_details":"[Object]","media_type":"image","mime_type":"image/jpeg","slug":"fb_img_1783239986189","source_url":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/FB_IMG_1783239986189.jpg","title":"[Object]","type":"attachment"}],"wp:term":[["[Object]"],[]]},"_links":{"about":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/types/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/users/2"}],"collection":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts"}],"curies":[{"href":"https://api.w.org/{rel}","name":"wp","templated":true}],"predecessor-version":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/120/revisions/121","id":121}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/comments?post=120"}],"self":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/120","targetHints":"[Object]"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/120/revisions"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media?parent=120"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media/95"}],"wp:term":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/categories?post=120","taxonomy":"category"},{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/tags?post=120","taxonomy":"post_tag"}]},"author":2,"categories":[1],"class_list":["post-120","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-news"],"comment_status":"closed","content":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p><strong>AppClick <a href=\"http://appclick.ng\">Technology</a> has taken another bold step in advancing Nigeria&#8217;s digital economy with the official unveiling of the corporate headquarters of AppClick ICT Academy in Ibadan, Oyo State, a landmark milestone that reinforces the company&#8217;s commitment to developing world-class technology talents and driving digital transformation across Africa.</strong></p>\n<p>The grand unveiling was accompanied by the graduation of over 60 students who successfully completed intensive professional training in high-demand digital skills, marking another significant achievement in AppClick&#8217;s mission to bridge the technology skills gap and prepare young Nigerians for the future of work.</p>\n<p>Held at the academy&#8217;s state-of-the-art headquarters in Old Bodija, Ibadan, the event attracted leading figures from government, academia, the technology ecosystem and the business community.</p>\n<p>The occasion also featured the maiden edition of the AppClick Technology Summit, themed &#8220;Navigating the Digital Revolution,&#8221; providing a platform for meaningful conversations on innovation, artificial intelligence, entrepreneurship and emerging opportunities in the global digital economy.</p>\n<p>Adding excitement to the event was a highly competitive Hackathon, where brilliant innovators showcased practical solutions to real-world challenges. Winners and runners-up were rewarded with cash prizes, underscoring AppClick&#8217;s commitment to nurturing creativity, rewarding excellence and encouraging problem-solving through technology.</p>\n<p>Speaking during the unveiling ceremony, the Founder and Chief Executive Officer of AppClick Technology, Seyi Adeleke, described the establishment of the academy&#8217;s headquarters as the fulfilment of a vision to position Ibadan as one of Nigeria&#8217;s emerging technology hubs.</p>\n<p>Seyi Adeleke is a Nigerian technology entrepreneur, digital innovation advocate and the Founder/Chief Executive Officer of AppClick Technology and AppClick ICT Academy. He is passionate about empowering young people with in-demand digital skills and leveraging technology to solve real-world problems.</p>\n<p>Through AppClick ICT Academy, Adeleke has championed digital capacity building by training thousands of students, entrepreneurs and professionals in fields such as Data Analytics, Digital Marketing, UI/UX Design, Python Programming and Mobile App Engineering. Under his leadership, the academy has grown into one of the emerging technology training institutions in Southwest Nigeria, with a vision to expand across Africa.</p>\n<p>Known for his commitment to innovation, youth empowerment and entrepreneurship, Adeleke has built a thriving technology ecosystem that combines education, mentorship and practical industry experience. His initiatives continue to create opportunities for aspiring tech professionals while contributing to Nigeria&#8217;s growing digital economy.</p>\n<p>Beyond technology, Seyi Adeleke is a sought-after speaker and mentor, inspiring young innovators to embrace lifelong learning, creativity and excellence in an increasingly digital world.</p>\n<p>According to him, AppClick ICT Academy was founded with a clear mission, to empower students, graduates, entrepreneurs and working professionals with practical, industry-relevant digital skills that can compete in today&#8217;s global technology ecosystem.</p>\n<p>&#8220;Our goal has never been just to train people,&#8221; Adeleke noted. &#8220;It is to build innovators, create problem-solvers, develop entrepreneurs and raise technology professionals who can compete anywhere in the world while contributing meaningfully to Africa&#8217;s digital economy.&#8221;</p>\n<p>He revealed that the academy has already trained more than 3,000 students in various technology disciplines since its inception, with graduates making remarkable strides across different sectors. Adeleke further disclosed that AppClick plans to expand its footprint across Nigeria and several African countries within the next five years, making quality technology education more accessible to thousands of aspiring digital professionals.</p>\n<p>The graduating students received professional diplomas in sought-after technology fields, including Data Analytics, Digital Marketing, UI/UX Design, Python Programming and Mobile App Engineering: skills that continue to rank among the most in-demand globally.</p>\n<p>The official unveiling of the headquarters was performed by the Director-General of the National Institute for Policy and Strategic Studies (NIPSS), Professor Ayo Omotayo, who applauded AppClick Technology for investing in human capital development and creating opportunities for young Nigerians to thrive in the digital age.</p>\n<p>The newly unveiled AppClick ICT Academy headquarters is a magnificent three-storey edifice that reflects the institution&#8217;s vision of delivering world-class digital education. Designed with modern architecture and equipped with cutting-edge technology, the sprawling facility boasts fully automated systems that create a smart, efficient and conducive learning environment.</p>\n<p>The building houses spacious, technology-enabled classrooms, advanced training laboratories, collaborative workspaces, conference rooms and administrative offices, all thoughtfully designed to support innovation, creativity and hands-on learning. Every classroom is fitted with modern digital facilities to provide students with an immersive learning experience that meets global standards.</p>\n<p>More than just a physical structure, the headquarters represents AppClick Technology&#8217;s commitment to excellence, innovation and the future of technology education. It stands as a landmark hub where aspiring digital professionals can acquire industry-relevant skills, collaborate on groundbreaking ideas and prepare for opportunities in the global digital economy.</p>\n<p>Omotayo commended Adeleke&#8217;s vision, describing the academy as a model for private-sector investment in education and innovation. He encouraged the graduating students to embrace continuous learning, resilience and networking, stressing that long-term success in technology depends more on adaptability and the willingness to learn than on one&#8217;s academic background.</p>\n<p>The summit also featured insightful presentations from respected industry leaders. The Chief Executive Officer of Cardify Africa, Tunde Buremo, challenged participants to identify pressing societal problems and leverage technology to create impactful solutions capable of transforming lives and businesses.</p>\n<p>Senior Site Reliability Engineer at Credit Direct Limited (FCMB Group), Babajide Dawodu, spoke on the growing influence of Artificial Intelligence, urging participants to embrace AI responsibly as a productivity tool that complements, rather than replaces, human expertise.</p>\n<p>Also speaking, Dotun Amujo, Senior Platform Engineer at FCMB Group, shared practical insights on building sustainable careers in technology, encouraging aspiring professionals to remain committed to continuous learning, innovation and excellence.</p>\n<p>Beyond the speeches and celebrations, the event reflected AppClick Technology&#8217;s broader vision of building a thriving innovation ecosystem where education, mentorship, entrepreneurship and technological advancement converge to produce globally competitive talent.</p>\n<p>As digital transformation continues to reshape industries worldwide, AppClick ICT Academy is positioning itself as more than just a training institution, it is emerging as a catalyst for innovation, a talent development hub and a launchpad for the next generation of African technology leaders.</p>\n<p>With a growing community of over 3,000 trained professionals, modern learning facilities, experienced instructors and ambitious expansion plans across Africa, AppClick Technology is steadily redefining digital education and demonstrating that world-class technology talent can be developed from Nigeria for the global marketplace.</p>\n<p>For AppClick Technology, the unveiling of its Ibadan headquarters is not merely the opening of a new building, it represents the beginning of a bigger vision to empower lives, inspire innovation and shape the future of Africa&#8217;s digital economy, one student at a time.</p>\n<p>OYO <a href=\"http://oyonews.com.ng\">NEWS</a></p>\n"},"date":"2026-07-06T14:43:42","date_gmt":"2026-07-06T14:43:42","excerpt":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>AppClick Technology has taken another bold step in advancing Nigeria&#8217;s digital economy with the official unveiling of the corporate headquarters of AppClick ICT Academy in…</p>\n"},"featured_media":95,"format":"standard","guid":{"rendered":"https://oyonews.com.ng/?p=120"},"id":120,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/appclick-ict-academy-sets-new-standard-in-digital-skills-development-with-unveiling-of-ibadan-headquarters/","meta":{"fifu_image_alt":"","fifu_image_url":"","footnotes":""},"modified":"2026-07-06T14:43:42","modified_gmt":"2026-07-06T14:43:42","ping_status":"open","slug":"appclick-ict-academy-sets-new-standard-in-digital-skills-development-with-unveiling-of-ibadan-headquarters","status":"publish","sticky":false,"tags":[],"template":"","title":{"rendered":"AppClick ICT Academy Sets New Standard in Digital Skills Development with Unveiling of Ibadan Headquarters"},"type":"post"},{"_embedded":{"author":[{"_links":"[Object]","avatar_urls":"[Object]","description":"","id":1,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/author/josebek/","name":"josebek","slug":"josebek","url":"https://oyonews.com.ng"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"_links":"[Object]","alt_text":"","author":1,"caption":"[Object]","date":"2026-07-05T19:40:26","featured_media":0,"id":118,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/oyebanji-visits-freed-ekiti-kidnap-victims-thanks-tinubu-security-agencies/img_8864/#main","media_details":"[Object]","media_type":"image","mime_type":"image/webp","slug":"img_8864","source_url":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/IMG_8864.webp","title":"[Object]","type":"attachment"}],"wp:term":[["[Object]"],[]]},"_links":{"about":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/types/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/users/1"}],"collection":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts"}],"curies":[{"href":"https://api.w.org/{rel}","name":"wp","templated":true}],"predecessor-version":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/117/revisions/119","id":119}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/comments?post=117"}],"self":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/117","targetHints":"[Object]"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/117/revisions"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media?parent=117"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media/118"}],"wp:term":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/categories?post=117","taxonomy":"category"},{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/tags?post=117","taxonomy":"post_tag"}]},"author":1,"categories":[1],"class_list":["post-117","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-news"],"comment_status":"closed","content":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>Ekiti State Governor Biodun Oyebanji on Sunday visited the victims of the Eda Oniyo kidnapping at the Ekiti State University Teaching Hospital, Ado Ekiti, where they are receiving medical attention following their release from captivity after about 66 days.</p>\n<p>The victims, comprising women and children, regained their freedom in the early hours of Saturday after they were abducted during a programme at a Christ Apostolic Church in Eda Oniyo on April 28.</p>\n<p>During the attack on the church, a pastor was killed, while 16 worshippers were marched into the bush by the kidnappers. One of the victims died in captivity, while the remaining 15 regained their freedom on Saturday.</p>\n<p>The governor, who visited each of the victims at the hospital’s emergency and children’s wards, expressed delight over their release, saying he was pleased that they were stable and responding well to treatment</p>\n<div id=\"show360playvid\" class=\"360playvidUnit\">\n<div class=\"cleanslate360pv\">\n<div>\n<p>Oyebanji said, “I am happy that they are back. I am happy that the little child who was unconscious yesterday (Saturday) is now conscious. All of them are stable, and they are responding to medical treatment. I commend the staff of EKSUTH for a job well done.</p>\n<p>“Since yesterday (Saturday), we brought them here to take care of them and ensure that they are medically sound. I have instructed that the state will bear the cost of their treatment and everything that happens between now and when they are discharged. I will also engage psychologists to counsel them with a view to reintegrating them with their family members.”</p>\n<p>The governor thanked God for the victims’ release, appreciated President Bola Tinubu for his support, and commended the efforts of security agencies, including members of the Hunters’ Association.</p>\n<p>He said, “I must particularly place on record my commendation for the efforts of members of the Eda Oniyo Progressives Association, the Christian Association of Nigeria and other well-meaning indigenes of Ekiti State who supported us in ensuring that the kidnap victims were finally released.”</p>\n<p>Oyebanji, however, advised residents against politicising security issues.</p>\n<p>“We should be our brothers’ keepers. The safety of every Ekiti indigene should be our concern, and there should be a limit to politics.</p>\n<p>“I will advise our people that when things like this happen, they should not weaponise them or play politics with them. It was so shocking that some people decided to play politics with this unfortunate incident,” he said.</p>\n<p>Recall that the governor had on Saturday directed the management of EKSUTH to provide comprehensive medical screening and treatment for the victims.</p>\n<p>According to a statement issued in Ado Ekiti on Saturday by the Special Adviser to the Governor on Media, Yinka Oyebode, the governor also instructed the State Ministry of Health and Human Services to take full responsibility for overseeing the victims’ welfare and well-being, with the state government covering all medical expenses incurred during their treatment.</p>\n<p>Oyebode added that the freed victims, who are currently receiving care at the teaching hospital, “will remain under medical supervision until they are certified fit for reintegration with their families.”</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"cleanslate360pv\">\n<div>\n<div></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n"},"date":"2026-07-05T19:40:37","date_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:40:37","excerpt":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>Ekiti State Governor Biodun Oyebanji on Sunday visited the victims of the Eda Oniyo kidnapping at the Ekiti State University Teaching Hospital, Ado Ekiti, where…</p>\n"},"featured_media":118,"format":"standard","guid":{"rendered":"https://oyonews.com.ng/?p=117"},"id":117,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/oyebanji-visits-freed-ekiti-kidnap-victims-thanks-tinubu-security-agencies/","meta":{"fifu_image_alt":"","fifu_image_url":"","footnotes":""},"modified":"2026-07-05T19:40:37","modified_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:40:37","ping_status":"open","slug":"oyebanji-visits-freed-ekiti-kidnap-victims-thanks-tinubu-security-agencies","status":"publish","sticky":false,"tags":[],"template":"","title":{"rendered":"Oyebanji visits freed Ekiti kidnap victims, thanks Tinubu, security agencies"},"type":"post"},{"_embedded":{"author":[{"_links":"[Object]","avatar_urls":"[Object]","description":"","id":1,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/author/josebek/","name":"josebek","slug":"josebek","url":"https://oyonews.com.ng"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"_links":"[Object]","alt_text":"","author":7777777777,"caption":"[Object]","date":"2026-07-05T21:35:59","featured_media":0,"id":115,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/buharis-greatest-legacy-is-investment-in-education-aisha//#main","media_details":"[Object]","media_type":"image","mime_type":"image/jpeg","slug":"","source_url":"https://punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/1005665089_1783271997.webp","title":"[Object]","type":"attachment"}],"wp:term":[["[Object]"],[]]},"_links":{"about":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/types/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/users/1"}],"collection":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts"}],"curies":[{"href":"https://api.w.org/{rel}","name":"wp","templated":true}],"predecessor-version":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/114/revisions/116","id":116}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/comments?post=114"}],"self":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/114","targetHints":"[Object]"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/114/revisions"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media?parent=114"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media/115"}],"wp:term":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/categories?post=114","taxonomy":"category"},{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/tags?post=114","taxonomy":"post_tag"}]},"author":1,"categories":[1],"class_list":["post-114","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-news"],"comment_status":"closed","content":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>Former First Lady, Hajiya Aisha Muhammadu Buhari, on Sunday said the greatest legacy of her late husband, former President Muhammadu Buhari, was his unwavering commitment to education and human capital development.</p>\n<p>She said this as she performed the groundbreaking ceremony for a new Computer Science Complex at Al-Qalam University, Katsina.</p>\n<p>The project is being financed from the proceeds of the book From Soldier to Statesman: The Legacy of Muhammadu Buhari, published to document the life and public service of the late Nigerian leader.</p>\n<p>Addressing dignitaries at the ceremony, Aisha Buhari described the initiative as a practical demonstration of the family’s resolve to preserve Buhari’s legacy by investing in knowledge and innovation rather than</p>\n<p>“Today marks far more than the commencement of a new building; it marks the beginning of a vision that equips future generations with the knowledge and skills required to thrive in an increasingly digital world,” she said.</p>\n<p>She noted that the Computer Science Complex would remain “a lasting testament to the enduring legacy of our late leader, General Muhammadu Buhari, whose life was defined by duty, discipline, integrity and unwavering commitment to national development.”</p>\n<p>The former First Lady further said converting the proceeds of the Buhari legacy book into an educational facility reflected the belief that “the greatest memorial we can build is not merely one of concrete and steel, but one that nurtures knowledge, character and opportunity for generations yet unborn.”</p>\n<p>She added that as technology increasingly drives global development, countries investing in computing, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, software engineering and digital innovation would shape the future.</p>\n<p>Offering prayers for the institution, she said she hoped every lecture delivered in the complex would inspire excellence, while every graduate would combine integrity with intelligence in advancing the development of Katsina State, Nigeria and the African continent.</p>\n<p>The Vice-Chancellor of Al-Qalam University, Prof. Nasiru Musa Yauri, described the project as a landmark intervention that would transform the teaching and learning of computing-related disciplines in the institution.</p>\n<p>“It is very dear to our hearts, a project that promises to bring unquantifiable benefits to our community,” Yauri said.</p>\n<p>He disclosed that the complex would comprise two 150-seat lecture halls, a 200-seat auditorium, three computer laboratories equipped with 50 computers each, offices for 20 academic staff with furniture and computers, alongside other supporting facilities.</p>\n<p>According to him, although Computer Science has been offered at the university since 2005, the institution had never had dedicated facilities of such magnitude.</p>\n<p>“This is the first time we are having full facilities for training our students in Computer Science, Information Technology, Cybersecurity and Software Engineering,” he said, adding that the intervention would significantly strengthen practical learning, research and innovation.</p>\n<p>Katsina State Governor Dikko Umaru Radda, represented at the ceremony by his deputy, Faruk Lawal Jobe, commended the Buhari family for sustaining the late president’s ideals through investments in education.</p>\n<p>“This edifice is a remarkable achievement. It is contributing towards education and knowledge, and there is no greater investment than one that enlightens society,” the governor said.</p>\n<p>The governor also praised Aisha Buhari for championing educational causes after the passing of the former president.</p>\n<p>“We thank you most sincerely for championing the cause of education even after the demise of our father, Muhammadu Buhari, because this is something he stood for,” he added.</p>\n<p>The ceremony attracted government officials, traditional rulers, academics, development partners, members of the university community and other invited guests.</p>\n<p>When completed, the Computer Science Complex is expected to strengthen Al-Qalam University’s digital infrastructure, improve research and practical training in computing disciplines, and reinforce the institution’s position as one of northern Nigeria’s emerging centres for technology education.</p>\n"},"date":"2026-07-05T19:35:59","date_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:35:59","excerpt":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>Former First Lady, Hajiya Aisha Muhammadu Buhari, on Sunday said the greatest legacy of her late husband, former President Muhammadu Buhari, was his unwavering commitment…</p>\n"},"featured_media":115,"format":"standard","guid":{"rendered":"https://oyonews.com.ng/?p=114"},"id":114,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/buharis-greatest-legacy-is-investment-in-education-aisha/","meta":{"fifu_image_alt":"","fifu_image_url":"https://punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/1005665089_1783271997.webp","footnotes":""},"modified":"2026-07-05T19:35:59","modified_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:35:59","ping_status":"open","slug":"buharis-greatest-legacy-is-investment-in-education-aisha","status":"publish","sticky":false,"tags":[],"template":"","title":{"rendered":"Buhari’s greatest legacy is investment in education — Aisha"},"type":"post"},{"_embedded":{"author":[{"_links":"[Object]","avatar_urls":"[Object]","description":"","id":1,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/author/josebek/","name":"josebek","slug":"josebek","url":"https://oyonews.com.ng"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"_links":"[Object]","alt_text":"","author":1,"caption":"[Object]","date":"2026-07-05T19:31:48","featured_media":0,"id":112,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/state-police-bill-endorsed-by-84-senators-senate/img_8863/#main","media_details":"[Object]","media_type":"image","mime_type":"image/png","slug":"img_8863","source_url":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/IMG_8863.png","title":"[Object]","type":"attachment"}],"wp:term":[["[Object]","[Object]"],[]]},"_links":{"about":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/types/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/users/1"}],"collection":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts"}],"curies":[{"href":"https://api.w.org/{rel}","name":"wp","templated":true}],"predecessor-version":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/111/revisions/113","id":113}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/comments?post=111"}],"self":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/111","targetHints":"[Object]"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/111/revisions"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media?parent=111"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media/112"}],"wp:term":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/categories?post=111","taxonomy":"category"},{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/tags?post=111","taxonomy":"post_tag"}]},"author":1,"categories":[1,5],"class_list":["post-111","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-news","category-politics"],"comment_status":"closed","content":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>The Senate on Sunday defended the passage of the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (Alteration) (State Police) Bill, 2026, insisting that 84 of the 109 senators voted in support of the bill during its clause-by-clause consideration, describing the outcome as evidence of broad bipartisan backing for the constitutional amendment.</p>\n<p>Defending the Senate’s decision in a statement issued by his media office, Senate Leader, Senator Opeyemi Bamidele, said the proposal was the product of years of consultations, public hearings and stakeholder engagements across the country.</p>\n<p>Bamidele also dismissed claims that the proposal was driven by partisan interests, insisting that it emerged from extensive consultations with the Executive, the Nigeria Governors’ Forum, the Conference of Speakers of State Legislatures and the leadership of the Nigeria Police.</p>\n<p>He noted that public hearings conducted across the six geopolitical zones in July 2025 produced overwhelming support for state police.</p>\n<p>He said, “The state police bill was subjected to intense debates at the Senate and House of Representatives. Even though the All Progressives Congress is the majority party, opposition legislators actively took part in the process that approved the state police initiative.</p>\n<p>“They exercised their discretion in favour of the proposal, mainly in the national interest and not on a parochial basis. In the Senate, for instance, 84 out of 109 members voted clause by clause in support of the Bill. This accounted for 77.06 per cent approval at the Senate alone.”</p>\n<p>The clarification comes amid growing debate over the proposed decentralisation of policing, with supporters arguing that state police will improve security at the grassroots, while critics fear governors could misuse the outfit to intimidate political opponents.</p>\n<p>He explained that under the proposal, the federal police would retain responsibility for the protection of federal institutions, the Federal Capital Territory, counter-terrorism, organised crime, cybercrime, border security, arms trafficking, interstate criminal activities and other national security matters.</p>\n<p>The state police, he said, would enforce state laws, maintain public order, prevent crime within their jurisdictions and protect lives and property.</p>\n<p>The Senate Leader said the bill also provides safeguards to prevent abuse of state police by governors.</p>\n<p>He explained that while governors may nominate commissioners of police, appointments would be subject to recommendations by the National Police Council and approval by a two-thirds majority of the relevant state House of Assembly.</p>\n<p>The lawmaker further stated that the National Police Council would oversee overall policy, funding and appointments, while a State Police Service Commission would operate independently of governors in regulating state police operations.</p>\n"},"date":"2026-07-05T19:32:07","date_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:32:07","excerpt":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>The Senate on Sunday defended the passage of the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (Alteration) (State Police) Bill, 2026, insisting that 84 of…</p>\n"},"featured_media":112,"format":"standard","guid":{"rendered":"https://oyonews.com.ng/?p=111"},"id":111,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/state-police-bill-endorsed-by-84-senators-senate/","meta":{"fifu_image_alt":"","fifu_image_url":"","footnotes":""},"modified":"2026-07-05T19:32:07","modified_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:32:07","ping_status":"open","slug":"state-police-bill-endorsed-by-84-senators-senate","status":"publish","sticky":false,"tags":[],"template":"","title":{"rendered":"State police bill endorsed by 84 senators – Senate"},"type":"post"},{"_embedded":{"author":[{"_links":"[Object]","avatar_urls":"[Object]","description":"","id":1,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/author/josebek/","name":"josebek","slug":"josebek","url":"https://oyonews.com.ng"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"_links":"[Object]","alt_text":"","author":7777777777,"caption":"[Object]","date":"2026-07-05T21:27:29","featured_media":0,"id":109,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/zamfara-wont-negotiate-with-bandits-says-lawal//#main","media_details":"[Object]","media_type":"image","mime_type":"image/jpeg","slug":"","source_url":"https://punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Zamfara-gov.webp","title":"[Object]","type":"attachment"}],"wp:term":[["[Object]"],[]]},"_links":{"about":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/types/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/users/1"}],"collection":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts"}],"curies":[{"href":"https://api.w.org/{rel}","name":"wp","templated":true}],"predecessor-version":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/108/revisions/110","id":110}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/comments?post=108"}],"self":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/108","targetHints":"[Object]"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/108/revisions"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media?parent=108"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/media/109"}],"wp:term":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/categories?post=108","taxonomy":"category"},{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://oyonews.com.ng/wp-json/wp/v2/tags?post=108","taxonomy":"post_tag"}]},"author":1,"categories":[1],"class_list":["post-108","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-news"],"comment_status":"closed","content":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>Governor Dauda Lawal of Zamfara State has said his administration will never negotiate with bandits but will sustain military operations until they surrender or face the full weight of the law.</p>\n<p>The governor made the declaration through his Chief of Staff, Mukhtar Musa, at the inauguration of the Association of Zamfara State Indigenes Residing in Kaduna secretariat on Sunday.</p>\n<p>The event also featured the distribution of food items to less-privileged members of the association in Kaduna.</p>\n<p>Musa said the Lawal administration remained committed to ending insecurity through sustained collaboration with security agencies across the state.</p>\n<p>He said the government would continue providing logistics, equipment, ammunition and other operational support to security forces fighting criminal elements.</p>\n<p>“The governor will never negotiate with bandits. Those willing to surrender should do so or face the law,” he said.</p>\n<p>He said the government would continue the offensive against bandits until lasting peace was restored across Zamfara.</p>\n<p>Musa said intelligence from residents remained critical to winning the fight against insecurity.</p>\n<p>He urged citizens to promptly report suspicious movements and activities to security agencies for immediate action.</p>\n<p>He assured members that the governor remained committed to delivering meaningful development and restoring peace across Zamfara.</p>\n<p>Musa described the gathering as important, saying it reflected unity among Zamfara indigenes living outside their home state.</p>\n<p>He commended the organisers for successfully establishing the association’s state secretariat in Kaduna.</p>\n<p>He assured members that Gov. Lawal would visit the association in the future to further strengthen relations.</p>\n<p>According to him, the governor is a progressive leader committed to supporting initiatives that promote unity and development among Zamfara indigenes.</p>\n"},"date":"2026-07-05T19:27:29","date_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:27:29","excerpt":{"protected":false,"rendered":"<p>Governor Dauda Lawal of Zamfara State has said his administration will never negotiate with bandits but will sustain military operations until they surrender or face…</p>\n"},"featured_media":109,"format":"standard","guid":{"rendered":"https://oyonews.com.ng/?p=108"},"id":108,"link":"https://oyonews.com.ng/zamfara-wont-negotiate-with-bandits-says-lawal/","meta":{"fifu_image_alt":"","fifu_image_url":"https://punchng.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Zamfara-gov.webp","footnotes":""},"modified":"2026-07-05T19:27:29","modified_gmt":"2026-07-05T19:27:29","ping_status":"open","slug":"zamfara-wont-negotiate-with-bandits-says-lawal","status":"publish","sticky":false,"tags":[],"template":"","title":{"rendered":"Zamfara won’t negotiate with bandits, says Lawal"},"type":"post"}]
